Package: distributionalDensity
Title: Prevalence, Burstiness, Position, and Dispersion of Verbal Behavior
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R:
    person("David", "Markowitz", , "davidmmarkowitz@gmail.com", role = c("aut", "cre"))
Description: Computes the four distributional density parameters of a
    psychologically-consequential language dimension within a text:
    prevalence (how much of the dimension is present), burstiness (how
    clustered its occurrences are, using the finite-size-corrected
    estimator of Kim and Jo (2016) <doi:10.1103/PhysRevE.94.032311>
    built on Goh and Barabasi (2008) <doi:10.1209/0295-5075/81/48002>;
    this is the raw burstiness formula that aligns with prior work),
    position (the mean normalized placement of occurrences in the text),
    and dispersion (the spread of occurrences around their mean position,
    normalized by the maximum attainable spread at that mean). Burstiness
    naturally grows less reliable as text length and occurrence count of
    the language dimension shrink, even after the finite-size correction;
    the package therefore offers a standardize option that normalizes
    burstiness against a simulated null of random occurrence placement at
    the same text length and occurrence count, removing this residual
    dependence so burstiness scores are comparable across texts of
    different lengths. Together these parameters describe how a verbal
    category is distributed across a disclosure rather than only how
    frequently it occurs, extending prevalence-based text analysis with a
    within-text analogue of Whole Trait Theory's density distributions
    (Fleeson, 2001) <doi:10.1037/0022-3514.80.6.1011>.
